      "paperId": "5c6e403121bd37b46107b82dc3cd4c1311202c09",
      "title": "Modeling Chinese Learners\u2019 Flow Experience in Intercultural English Teaching Based on BP Neural Networks",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Xuxia Dong",
          "authorId": "2346455981"
        },
        {
          "name": "Benjie Ding",
          "authorId": "2284417022"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2025,
      "abstract": "Flow theory emphasizes learners\u2019 optimal psychological state during task engagement, which plays a crucial role in promoting performance and satisfaction in foreign language learning. This study constructed two Back Propagation (BP) neural network prediction models to investigate Chinese English-as-a-foreign-language (EFL) learners\u2019 flow experience and the consequences in the context of intercultural English teaching. Based on a theoretical framework integrating flow antecedents, flow experience, and flow consequences, data were collected from 456 non-English major college students through structured questionnaires and writing tasks. The first BP neural network model was trained to predict flow experience from seven variables of flow antecedents, while the second model was developed to predict five learning outcome variables\u2014knowledge, skill, attitude, satisfaction, and achievement\u2014based on learners\u2019 flow experience. The Levenberg\u2013Marquardt (LM) algorithm was applied to ensure high training efficiency and prediction accuracy. Results showed strong correlations between flow antecedents and flow experience, as well as between flow experience and flow consequences. Both models demonstrated good predictive performance, with regression coefficients above 0.82 in training and testing phases. Although certain individual prediction errors were noted, most deviations remained within acceptable ranges. This two-stage modeling approach enhances interpretability, improves generalization, and provides valuable guidance for optimizing intercultural English instruction through flow-based educational design.",
      "citationCount": 0,
      "doi": "10.1145/3764206.3764300",
